1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,545
Close this book.

2
00:00:01,765 --> 00:00:05,028
Try to remember what you just read.

3
00:00:05,248 --> 00:00:07,911
Harder than rereading it, right?

4
00:00:08,131 --> 00:00:10,501
That struggle is the point.

5
00:00:10,721 --> 00:00:14,631
It is called retrieval practice, and it is

6
00:00:14,651 --> 00:00:18,382
one of the best-studied ways to make learning

7
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:20,802
Retrieval practice means pulling information

8
00:00:18,412 --> 00:00:18,901
stick.

9
00:00:20,832 --> 00:00:24,804
out of your memory instead of putting it in

10
00:00:24,824 --> 00:00:25,287
again.

11
00:00:25,507 --> 00:00:28,384
Rereading feels productive because the page

12
00:00:28,404 --> 00:00:29,379
looks familiar.

13
00:00:29,599 --> 00:00:33,542
But familiarity is not the same as being able

14
00:00:33,562 --> 00:00:36,906
to recall it later, without the book.

15
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:39,773
The evidence goes back to 1909, but a clear

16
00:00:39,793 --> 00:00:41,886
modern test came in 2006.

17
00:00:42,106 --> 00:00:45,207
Two researchers asked students to either reread

18
00:00:45,227 --> 00:00:47,715
a passage or practice recalling it.

19
00:00:47,935 --> 00:00:51,182
One week later, the recall group remembered

20
00:00:51,202 --> 00:00:52,082
substantially more.

21
00:00:52,302 --> 00:00:55,662
A 2017 review of dozens of experiments found

22
00:00:55,682 --> 00:00:58,719
the same pattern across ages and subjects.

23
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:59,603
Why does struggling to recall help?

24
00:00:59,823 --> 00:01:03,401
Each time you pull a memory out, your brain

25
00:01:03,421 --> 00:01:06,926
rebuilds the path to it, and the path gets

26
00:01:06,946 --> 00:01:08,841
easier to find next time.

27
00:01:09,061 --> 00:01:11,414
Psychologists call this a desirable difficulty.

28
00:01:11,634 --> 00:01:15,422
The effort is not a sign of failure, it is

29
00:01:15,442 --> 00:01:18,094
part of how the memory gets stronger.

30
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:20,303
You can use this in three ways.

31
00:01:20,523 --> 00:01:24,038
Write practice questions and answer them from

32
00:01:24,058 --> 00:01:24,578
memory.

33
00:01:24,798 --> 00:01:27,834
Make flashcards and grade yourself honestly.

34
00:01:28,054 --> 00:01:32,383
Or do a brain dump: close your notes and

35
00:01:32,403 --> 00:01:35,855
write down everything you remember about the

36
00:01:35,875 --> 00:01:39,008
topic, then check what you missed.

37
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:41,878
So the next time you study, skip the highlighter

38
00:01:41,898 --> 00:01:42,388
marathon.

39
00:01:42,608 --> 00:01:46,164
Close the book first and try to recall.

40
00:01:46,384 --> 00:01:50,171
When recall is hard, that is when learning

41
00:01:50,191 --> 00:01:51,072
is happening.
